<article class="env-remark" id="08U5">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08U5">Remark <span data-tag="08U5">15.88.12</span>.</a> Let $(K_ n)$ be an inverse system of objects of $D(\textit{Ab})$. Let $K = R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits K_ n$ be a derived limit of this system (see Derived Categories, Section <a href="/tag/08TB" data-tag="08TB">13.34</a>). Such a derived limit exists because $D(\textit{Ab})$ has countable products (Derived Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/07KC" data-tag="07KC">13.34.2</a>). By Lemma <a href="/tag/0CQ9" data-tag="0CQ9">15.88.11</a> we can also lift $(K_ n)$ to an object $M$ of $D(\textit{Ab}(\mathbf{N}))$. Then $K \cong R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M$ where $R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits $ is the functor (<a href="/tag/08U4" data-tag="08U4">15.88.1.1</a>) because $R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M$ is also a derived limit of the system $(K_ n)$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/07KX" data-tag="07KX">15.88.9</a>. Thus, although there may be many isomorphism classes of lifts $M$ of the system $(K_ n)$, the isomorphism type of $R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M$ is independent of the choice because it is isomorphic to the derived limit $K = R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits K_ n$ of the system. Thus we may apply results on $R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits $ proved in this section to derived limits. For example, for every $p \in \mathbf{Z}$ there is a canonical short exact sequence </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to R^1\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits H^{p - 1}(K_ n) \to H^ p(K) \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits H^ p(K_ n) \to 0  \]
</div>
  <p> because we may apply Lemma <a href="/tag/07KY" data-tag="07KY">15.88.10</a> to $M$. This can also been seen directly, without invoking the existence of $M$, by applying the argument of the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/07KY" data-tag="07KY">15.88.10</a> to the (defining) distinguished triangle $K \to \prod K_ n \to \prod K_ n \to K[1]$. </p>
</article>